          "explanation": "Harari emphasizes that Europe's political fragmentation made it impossible to suppress innovation everywhere at once. Galileo could publish in Holland after being silenced in Rome. Descartes worked in the Dutch Republic. Locke fled to Amsterdam. Newton's ideas spread despite religious resistance. By contrast, China and the Ottoman Empire had single centralized authorities that could shut down threatening ideas across vast territories. Fragmentation acted as a safety net for heterodox thought — a key institutional condition for the discovery of ignorance to spread.",
