          "explanation": "Harari uses an expanded, functional definition of religion: any system of norms grounded in unverifiable belief in a transcendent or superhuman order. Capitalism's faith that markets allocate optimally, that growth is intrinsically good, and that individual self-interest aggregates to collective benefit are held by billions without empirical proof and organize most modern decisions. Critics rightly note this stretches the term — by this definition, science and nationalism are also religions. Whether the analogy illuminates or obscures is a real debate. But the structural similarities Harari highlights are not invented.",
